  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nHeading into Saturday, ensemble guidance has backed off on shunting \noff our supply of monsoonal moisture as the closed low to our \nnorthwest begins to lift to the north and east. While not as \nimpressive as the previous couple of days, PWAT values are still \nexpected to range between the 85th and 95th percentiles relative to \nclimatology, keeping additional shower and thunderstorm chances in \nthe forecast. The bulk of the forcing for ascent looks to be from \nlate Saturday evening into the overnight hours, with an additional \nchance for some much needed rainfall. As it stands, ensemble \nguidance has 55-65% chances of all areas east of the I-25 corridor \nseeing at least 0.1\" of rain from midnight to noon on Sunday, with \nthe highest values across the Nebraska Panhandle. However the spread \nremains rather large, with the 25th percentile QPF being essentially \n0.01\"-0.05\", which wouldn't exactly do much to help our drought \nconditions. Forecast soundings across the Panhandle show skinny CAPE \nprofiles throughout the troposphere, PWAT values around the 70th \npercentile, and warm cloud layers up to ~3 km, so any showers and \nstorms are expected to be rather efficient rainfall producers. \nUnsurprisingly, high temperatures on Sunday will be rather cool \n(although not terribly far below average) in the 60s for SE Wyoming, \nand low 70s in the Panhandle. \n\nHeading into the work week, a warming trend is expected as flow is \nmore out of the WNW, resulting in drier conditions as another ridge \nbuilds to our west over the Great Basin. Expect temperatures to be \naround or slightly above seasonal values on Monday and Tuesday in the \nupper 60s to mid 70s (warmest temps across the Panhandle), and \nlittle to no chances for precipitation. \n\nOn Wednesday, ensemble guidance hints at the ridge building and \nshifting east, placing SE Wyoming and W Nebraska in more WSW flow \naloft, allowing for some return of monsoonal moisture. At this time, \nany monsoonal moisture surge doesn't look nearly as impressive as \nthe one in the short/medium range, but low precipitation chances look \nto return come mid next week.",
